        }
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.4,
      "why": "iterative is the smallest-area family (1 micro-rotation/cycle, shared datapath). It cannot meet 32 Msps throughput on its own (it produces 1 result every N+3 cycles), so it will likely violate the throughput constraint. However, it is included to map the low-area end of the Pareto front and to confirm infeasibility of the throughput constraint for this family. If the fleet merges fronts, the iterative designs will be dominated on throughput but may inform the area floor. data_width/n_iter ranges match unrolled_k for accuracy coverage."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec demands 32 Msps throughput and a 0.44 us p99 batch deadline for 32 simultaneous requests. Among my assigned families, only unrolled_k can plausibly meet both: with k=6-8 micro-rotations/cycle, the per-result latency is small (ceil(N/k)+3 cycles) and the batch of 32 sequential requests completes in 32*ceil(N/k) cycles, which at typical Fmax (150-300 MHz) fits within 0.44 us. iterative cannot meet 32 Msps (it needs N+3 cycles per result), so it will be infeasible on throughput, but I include it at 40% budget to map the area floor and confirm the infeasibility boundary. Accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) requires roughly data_width >= 14 and n_iter >= 12; I range data_width 14-18 and n_iter 12-18 to capture the trade-off between area and accuracy. angle_guard 0-2 and frac_guard 0-2 keep the search focused on low-area configurations while allowing enough guard bits for accuracy. rounding is included as a categorical choice since it affects both area and accuracy slightly. The 60/40 split favours unrolled_k because it is the only family that can satisfy the hard throughput and batch-latency constraints."
}

{
  "decision": "add_family",
  "rationale": "The iterative and unrolled_k families are fundamentally infeasible for the throughput constraint. The spec requires 32 MSPS, but the best observed throughput for iterative is 13.2 MSPS and for unrolled_k is 10.3 MSPS. Since iterative produces 1 result every N+3 cycles and unrolled_k every ceil(N/k)+3 cycles, their throughput is limited by latency. To meet 32 MSPS, we need a design with 1 result/cycle throughput, which requires the pipelined or pipelined_m families. Additionally, the accuracy constraint (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) is tight; the best accuracy seen so far is ~13.5 bits, which is close but we need to ensure the pipelined designs can hit this while maintaining the area objective. I will add the pipelined family to explore the 1-result/cycle regime.",
