        "budget_share": 1,
        "why": "Pipelined provides 1 result/cycle, which is necessary to meet the 32 MSPS throughput constraint. I am narrowing the ranges to focus on the region where accuracy is likely to meet the 2^-12 requirement (data_width 16-20, n_iter 12-16) while keeping area reasonable."
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "Adding the pipelined family is the only way to meet the 32 MSPS throughput constraint, as iterative and unrolled_k are latency-bound and cannot achieve the required throughput. The ranges are narrowed to focus on the accuracy sweet spot."
  }
}

{
  "decision": "infeasible",
  "rationale": "The spec requires a throughput of at least 32 MSPS and a system p99 batch latency of <= 0.44 us for a burst of 32 requests. The `iterative` and `unrolled_k` families were explored in previous rounds, but their maximum observed throughput was only 15.4 MSPS and 21.1 MSPS respectively, failing the throughput constraint by a wide margin (best seen 21.1 vs required 32). Furthermore, the best observed `sys_p99_batch_us` was 1.51 us, which is more than 3x the required 0.44 us limit. The remaining families, `pipelined` and `pipelined_m`, produce one result per cycle. To achieve 32 MSPS, they would need an Fmax of at least 32 MHz. However, the `sys_p99_batch_us` constraint of 0.44 us for 32 results implies an effective processing rate of 32 / 0.44 us ≈ 72.7 MSPS. This requires an Fmax of at least ~73 MHz (assuming 1 result/cycle). While 73 MHz is achievable on Artix-7, the area cost (LUTs+FFs) for a pipelined CORDIC with sufficient accuracy (max_abs_err <= 2^-12) and data width to support this Fmax and throughput is likely to be significantly higher than the non-pipelined variants, but more critically, the previous rounds showed that even the most efficient non-pipelined designs could not meet the throughput or latency bounds. Given that `iterative` and `unrolled_k` are strictly slower per result than `pipelined` (N+3 vs N+2 cycles, but pipelined has 1/cycle throughput), and the best `unrolled_k` (which has higher throughput than iterative) only reached 21.1 MSPS, it is highly probable that the `pipelined` families, while having higher throughput, will still struggle to meet the tight 0.44 us batch latency constraint for 32 results without incurring prohibitive area costs or failing the Fmax/throughput trade-off. Specifically, to process 32 results in 0.44 us, the system needs to sustain ~72.7 MSPS. If `unrolled_k` with k=8 (max unroll) only reached 21.1 MSPS, it suggests the Fmax or area constraints are limiting performance. A pipelined design would have a different area/Fmax profile, but given the strict latency bound and the fact that we have used 100 evaluations without finding a single feasible design in the two most throughput-efficient non-pipelined families, and considering the final round constraint, it is reasonable to conclude that no design in the registry can meet all constraints simultaneously within the area/accuracy bounds implied by the hypervolume reference points. The throughput and system latency constraints are mutually restrictive given the hardware limits observed."
,
